Relevant columns and their potential usage
trees.zipcode: key for joining with income data  
trees.boroname: borough name for grouping and final reporting  
trees.tree_id (or any other column) : counting number of trees per borough  
income_trees.zipcode: key for joining with tree records  
income_trees.Estimate_Median_income: filter ( > 0 ) so we only keep records with a valid median income  
income_trees.Estimate_Mean_income: filter ( > 0 ) and used for borough‑level average mean‑income calculation  

Filtering / joining conditions that may be useful
• trees.zipcode = income_trees.zipcode  
• income_trees.Estimate_Median_income > 0  
• income_trees.Estimate_Mean_income > 0  
• trees.boroname IS NOT NULL  

Ten exploratory → final SQL queries
```sql
-- 1. Inspect a handful of tree records with their ZIP codes and borough names
SELECT "tree_id", "zipcode", "boroname"
FROM "trees"
WHERE "boroname" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Look at the ZIP codes that have income data
SELECT DISTINCT "zipcode"
FROM "income_trees"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Verify the distinct borough names in the tree inventory
SELECT DISTINCT "boroname"
FROM "trees"
WHERE "boroname" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Preview the joined data set (tree + income) on ZIP code
SELECT t."tree_id",
       t."boroname",
       t."zipcode",
       i."Estimate_Median_income",
       i."Estimate_Mean_income"
FROM   "trees" AS t
JOIN   "income_trees" AS i
  ON   t."zipcode" = i."zipcode"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Same join but keep only rows with positive median & mean income
SELECT t."tree_id",
       t."boroname",
       t."zipcode",
       i."Estimate_Median_income",
       i."Estimate_Mean_income"
FROM   "trees" AS t
JOIN   "income_trees" AS i
  ON   t."zipcode" = i."zipcode"
WHERE  i."Estimate_Median_income" > 0
  AND  i."Estimate_Mean_income"  > 0
  AND  t."boroname" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Count trees per borough (no income filter yet)
SELECT t."boroname",
       COUNT(*) AS "tree_count"
FROM   "trees" AS t
WHERE  t."boroname" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY t."boroname"
ORDER  BY "tree_count" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Count trees per borough after applying income filters
SELECT t."boroname",
       COUNT(*) AS "tree_count"
FROM   "trees" AS t
JOIN   "income_trees" AS i
  ON   t."zipcode" = i."zipcode"
WHERE  i."Estimate_Median_income" > 0
  AND  i."Estimate_Mean_income"  > 0
  AND  t."boroname" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY t."boroname"
ORDER  BY "tree_count" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Retrieve only the three boroughs with the greatest tree counts (after filter)
SELECT t."boroname",
       COUNT(*) AS "tree_count"
FROM   "trees" AS t
JOIN   "income_trees" AS i
  ON   t."zipcode" = i."zipcode"
WHERE  i."Estimate_Median_income" > 0
  AND  i."Estimate_Mean_income"  > 0
  AND  t."boroname" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY t."boroname"
ORDER  BY "tree_count" DESC
LIMIT 3;
```

```sql
-- 9. Compute the average mean income for every borough (income filters already applied)
SELECT t."boroname",
       AVG(i."Estimate_Mean_income") AS "avg_mean_income"
FROM   "trees" AS t
JOIN   "income_trees" AS i
  ON   t."zipcode" = i."zipcode"
WHERE  i."Estimate_Median_income" > 0
  AND  i."Estimate_Mean_income"  > 0
  AND  t."boroname" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY t."boroname"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER:
-- Top‑3 boroughs by tree count (with valid income data) and their average mean income
SELECT t."boroname",
       COUNT(*)                              AS "tree_count",
       ROUND(AVG(i."Estimate_Mean_income"),1) AS "avg_mean_income"
FROM   "trees" AS t
JOIN   "income_trees" AS i
  ON   t."zipcode" = i."zipcode"
WHERE  i."Estimate_Median_income" > 0
  AND  i."Estimate_Mean_income"  > 0
  AND  t."boroname" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY t."boroname"
ORDER  BY "tree_count" DESC
LIMIT 3;
```
The last query returns exactly the three boroughs that contain the most trees (under the stated restrictions) together with the average mean income for each of those boroughs.
